.TH RFIND 1 "2026-10-14" "rfind 1.0.1" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
rfind \- search the filesystem instantly using the rfindd index
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B rfind
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.RI [ PATTERN ]
.RB [ \-\-regex | \-\-path ]
.RB [ \-\-under
.IR DIR ]
.RB [ \-\-largest | \-\-recent ]
.RB [ \-\-top
.IR N ]
.RB [ \-\-minutes | \-\-hours | \-\-days
.IR N
|
.B \-\-from
.I DATE
.B \-\-to
.IR DATE ]
.RI [ FILTERS ]
.br
.B rfind
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.B size
.RI [ DIR ]
.RB [ \-\-top
.IR N ]
.br
.B rfind
.BR status " [" \-\-watch "] | " stats " | " update " | " reload
.br
.B rfind config
.I SUBCOMMAND
.br
.B rfind completions
.I SHELL
.br
.B rfind uninstall
.RB [ \-\-purge ]
.RB [ \-\-yes ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B rfind
answers file-name, path, modification-time and disk-usage questions from an
index kept current by the
.BR rfindd (8)
daemon, instead of walking the disk like
.BR find (1).
Unlike
.BR locate (1),
the index is updated live from kernel filesystem events (fanotify, or inotify
as a fallback), so new, renamed and deleted files are reflected within
seconds.
.PP
The index lists every file name on the system, so it is readable by root only:
searches must be run with
.BR sudo (8).
Searching never modifies the index.
.SH SEARCH
.B rfind
takes an optional pattern plus any mix of selection, scope, sort and
filter flags. Flag order never matters.
.TP
.I PATTERN
Default: a case-sensitive glob matched against the basename
.RB ( *
matches any characters,
.B ?
one character). With
.B \-\-regex
the pattern is a regular expression (Rust
.B regex
syntax); with
.B \-\-path
it is a full-path glob (where
.B *
also matches
.BR / );
.B \-\-regex \-\-path
is a regex over the full path. The pattern may be omitted when selecting
with
.BR \-\-largest ,
.BR \-\-recent
or a time filter alone.
.TP
.BI \-\-under " DIR"
Only match entries inside the tree rooted at
.IR DIR .
.TP
.B \-\-largest
Sort results by size, biggest first. Combined with a pattern, returns the
biggest matches.
.TP
.B \-\-recent
Sort by modification time, newest first. Implied by any time filter below.
.TP
.BI \-\-top " N"
Keep the N best (or first) results.
.B \-\-limit
.I N
is a synonym.
.TP
.BR \-\-minutes " | " \-\-hours " | " \-\-days " " N
Only entries modified in the last
.I N
minutes/hours/days.
.TP
.B \-\-from
.I YYYY-MM-DD
.B \-\-to
.I YYYY-MM-DD
Date range in local time, inclusive.
.PP
Legacy subcommands
.RB ( "rfind name" " PATTERN,"
.B rfind path
.IR GLOB ,
.BR "rfind recent" ,
.BR "rfind largest" )
remain as aliases over the same logic for scripts and muscle memory.
.TP
.BI "rfind size " "\fR[\fPDIR\fR]\fP"
Disk usage of a directory tree (default
.BR / ),
computed from the indexed file sizes without touching the disk: the total
bytes, files and directories, and the
.B \-\-top
.I N
(default 20) largest direct children, like
.BR "du \-d1" .
.B du
is an alias.
Sizes are apparent sizes (file length), not allocated blocks.
.SS Filters
.TP
.BR \-\-type " " f | d
Only files or only directories.
.TP
.BI \-\-larger\-than " SIZE"
Only files of at least
.IR SIZE .
.TP
.BI \-\-smaller\-than " SIZE"
Only files of at most
.IR SIZE .
.TP
.BR \-l ", " \-\-long
Print size and modification time before each path.
.PP
.I SIZE
is a number of bytes with an optional binary suffix:
.BR K ,
.BR M ,
.B G
or
.B T
(powers of 1024), e.g.
.BR 500K ,
.BR 1.5G .
A trailing
.B B
or
.B iB
is accepted.
.SH DAEMON COMMANDS
.TP
.B status
Unified daemon and index state: whether rfindd is running, the current
phase, index freshness
.RB ( LIVE
when monitored, otherwise
.BR FRESH / RECENT / STALE
by last-scan age), entry counts, total indexed bytes, monitoring mode,
pending events, database size, and any periodic or new-mount scan in
flight on the background thread.
.B \-\-watch
refreshes every second until the daemon reports Ready.
.B health
is a backward-compatible alias.
.TP
.B stats
Entry counts, the most common file extensions, and the directories with the
most direct entries.
.TP
.B update
Rescan now: live roots immediately, periodic roots in the background
(sends SIGUSR1 to rfindd).
.TP
.B reload
Make rfindd re-read its configuration (sends SIGHUP).
Only needed after editing the file by hand;
.B rfind config
reloads automatically.
.SH CONFIGURATION COMMANDS
.B rfind config
reads and changes
.IR /etc/rfindd/config.toml ,
described in
.BR rfind.conf (5).
Changes keep the file's comments and formatting, are validated before
anything is written, and are written atomically.
Afterwards rfindd is told to reload: it removes entries that are now excluded
or outside every root and scans what was added, without rebuilding the index.
Add
.B \-\-no\-reload
to only write the file.
.TP
.BR "config show" " [" \-\-defaults ]
Print the file (with
.BR \-\-json :
the effective settings), or the built-in defaults.
.TP
.B config path
Print the config file's location.
.TP
.B config check
Validate the file: syntax, unknown keys, relative paths, conflicting roots.
.TP
.B config keys
List the settings understood by
.B get
and
.BR set ,
with current values and descriptions.
.TP
.BI "config get " KEY
Print one setting, e.g.
.BR scan.workers " or " scan.exclude .
.TP
.BI "config set " "KEY VALUE"
Change one setting, e.g.
.BR "rfind config set scan.workers 8" .
Booleans accept true/false, yes/no, on/off, 1/0.
.TP
.BI "config unset " KEY
Remove a setting from the file so its default applies.
.TP
.BR "config exclude add" | remove | list " [\fIPATH\fR...]"
Paths that are never indexed nor descended into.
.TP
.BR "config root add" | remove | list " [\fIPATH\fR...]"
Live roots: scanned, then kept current by fanotify or inotify.
.TP
.BR "config periodic add" " \fIPATH\fR... [" \-\-every " \fIHOURS\fR] | " remove " \fIPATH\fR... | " list
Periodic roots: rescanned every
.I HOURS
(default 12) in the background and never monitored.
Use them for filesystems whose changes the kernel can't report, such as
Windows drives under WSL2 or network shares.
.TP
.B config edit
Open the file in
.BR $VISUAL ,
.B $EDITOR
or
.BR vi ;
it is validated before saving, and an invalid edit can be corrected or
discarded.
.TP
.BR "config reset \-\-yes"
Replace the file with the defaults, keeping a
.I .bak
copy.
.PP
Paths are normalized
.RB ( . ,
.B ..
and trailing slashes are removed) and must be absolute.
Adding a path that doesn't exist yet is allowed, with a warning.
.SH OTHER COMMANDS
.TP
.BI "completions " SHELL
Print a completion script for
.BR bash ,
.BR zsh ,
.BR fish ,
.B elvish
or
.BR powershell .
The installer installs them for bash, zsh and fish.
.TP
.B uninstall
Stop and disable rfindd and remove the binaries, man pages, completions and
systemd unit.
.B \-\-purge
also deletes the index and the configuration.
Asks for confirmation unless
.BR \-\-yes .
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-json
Machine-readable output.
Searches print one JSON object per result (JSON Lines) with the keys
.BR path ,
.B type
.RB ( file " or " dir ),
.B size
(bytes, 0 for directories) and
.B mtime
(Unix seconds);
.B recent
adds
.B modified
(local time).
Other commands print a single object.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Help for any command.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Version.
.SH OUTPUT
Results go to standard output, one per line, sorted by path (by time for
.BR recent ,
by size for
.BR largest ).
The result count goes to standard error, so output can be piped.
Paths containing newlines are printed as-is; use
.B \-\-json
for unambiguous parsing.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Results found, or the command succeeded.
.TP
.B 1
A search found nothing.
.TP
.B 2
An error: invalid arguments, not root, no index, or an invalid configuration.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B RFIND_CONFIG
Use this configuration file instead of
.IR /etc/rfindd/config.toml .
.BR rfindd (8)
honors it too.
.TP
.BR VISUAL ", " EDITOR
Editor for
.BR "rfind config edit" .
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/rfindd/config.toml
Configuration; see
.BR rfind.conf (5).
.TP
.I /var/lib/rfindd/index.db
The index (SQLite, root-only).
.TP
.I /run/rfindd/progress.json
Daemon status read by
.BR "rfind status" .
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B sudo rfind passwd
Every entry named exactly
.BR passwd .
.TP
.B sudo rfind '*.conf' \-\-under /etc
Config files under /etc.
.TP
.B sudo rfind '^lib.*\e.so\e.[0-9]+$' \-\-regex
Versioned shared libraries.
.TP
.B sudo rfind '/home/*/.ssh/*' \-\-path
Everything in users' .ssh directories.
.TP
.B sudo rfind \-\-recent \-\-hours 2 \-\-type f \-l
Files changed in the last two hours, with size and time.
.TP
.B sudo rfind '*.py' \-\-largest \-\-top 20
Twenty biggest Python files on the system.
.TP
.B sudo rfind '*.iso' \-\-larger\-than 1G
Disk images of 1 GiB or more.
.TP
.B sudo rfind '*.log' \-\-recent \-\-hours 6 \-\-under /var \-\-top 10 \-l
Composition: ten most-recent /var logs from the last six hours, long form.
.TP
.B sudo rfind size /home \-\-top 10
Which home directories use the most space.
.TP
.B sudo rfind \-\-largest \-\-under /var \-\-top 10
The ten biggest files under /var.
.TP
.B sudo rfind '*.log' \-\-json | jq \-r 'select(.size > 1e8) | .path'
Logs over 100 MB, via jq.
.TP
.B sudo rfind config exclude add /home/moshe
Stop indexing a directory; its entries are removed right away.
.TP
.B sudo rfind config periodic add /mnt/nas \-\-every 24
Index a network share once a day.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR rfindd (8),
.BR rfind.conf (5),
.BR find (1),
.BR locate (1),
.BR plocate (1),
.BR du (1),
.BR fanotify (7),
.BR inotify (7)
.PP
https://github.com/YanivSaar/rfind
